1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:05,490
This is a black hole.

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:07,390
Nobody painted it.

3
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:09,620
Nobody photographed it.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,280
Every point of light you see was placed

5
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:13,580
there by a calculation.

6
00:00:15,370 --> 00:00:17,540
And the whole picture comes from one

7
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:18,220
equation.

8
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:20,980
A single line of math, that tells a ray of

9
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:21,930
light where to go.

10
00:00:22,770 --> 00:00:24,760
By the end of this video, you will be able

11
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:26,333
to read that equation.

12
00:00:26,387 --> 00:00:28,360
And you will know why this image looks the

13
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:29,040
way it does.

14
00:00:30,010 --> 00:00:32,070
Because it is stranger than it seems.

15
00:00:32,570 --> 00:00:34,540
Look at this bright arc, curving over the

16
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:34,930
top.

17
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,100
You would think it is a second ring of

18
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:39,090
gas, standing upright behind the hole.

19
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:40,753
It is not.

20
00:00:40,753 --> 00:00:43,020
There is only one flat disk here, and you

21
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:44,700
are seeing it in a way that should be

22
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:45,510
impossible.

23
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:48,667
To understand how, we will start with a

24
00:00:48,773 --> 00:00:50,880
single ray of light, and end by building

25
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:52,940
this image, one pixel at a time.

26
00:00:54,890 --> 00:00:56,630
Let us begin with a simpler question.

27
00:00:57,070 --> 00:00:58,280
Can light be trapped?

28
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:01,280
Throw a ball upward, and it falls back.

29
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,520
Throw it faster, and it climbs higher

30
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:05,067
before it returns.

31
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:06,800
But there is a speed at which it never

32
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:07,710
returns at all.

33
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:09,890
That is the escape velocity.

34
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:11,920
For the Earth, it is about eleven

35
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,280
kilometres every second.

36
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:15,260
The formula is short.

37
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:17,620
The escape velocity depends on the mass

38
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:19,620
you are leaving, and on how far you are

39
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:20,340
from its centre.

40
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:23,220
More mass, or less distance, and you need

41
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:23,810
more speed.

42
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:26,840
So here is a thought, first written down

43
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:28,220
in seventeen eighty three.

44
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:31,080
Squeeze a mass into a smaller and smaller

45
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:31,420
ball.

46
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:33,960
The escape velocity at its surface keeps

47
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:34,440
rising.

48
00:01:34,750 --> 00:01:36,840
At some point, it reaches the speed of

49
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:37,130
light.

50
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:40,140
Set the escape velocity equal to the speed

51
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:42,300
of light, solve for the radius, and you

52
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:42,950
get this.

53
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:46,140
Two times G times the mass, divided by the

54
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:47,230
speed of light squared.

55
00:01:47,590 --> 00:01:49,610
It is called the Schwarzschild radius.

56
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:52,480
For the Earth, that radius is nine

57
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:53,160
millimetres.

58
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:55,650
The whole planet, pressed into a marble.

59
00:01:56,070 --> 00:01:58,000
For the Sun, it is three kilometres.

60
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:00,660
Anything squeezed inside its own

61
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:03,300
Schwarzschild radius is a black hole.

62
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:05,690
And that sphere is the edge of no return.

63
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:08,600
But this argument has a problem.

64
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:10,603
It treats light like a thrown ball, and

65
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:12,030
light has no mass.

66
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:14,413
If you push the same reasoning further,

67
00:02:14,467 --> 00:02:16,280
and ask how much a passing ray of light

68
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:18,190
should bend, you do get an answer.

69
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:21,160
And that answer is wrong, by a factor of

70
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:22,150
exactly two.

71
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:23,480
Remember that number.

72
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:24,410
It comes back.

73
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:27,420
To get it right, we need a better idea of

74
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:28,460
what gravity is.

75
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:31,140
Here is the idea.

76
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:33,820
Light always travels in a straight line.

77
00:02:34,230 --> 00:02:34,800
Always.

78
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:36,580
Gravity does not pull on it.

79
00:02:37,450 --> 00:02:40,140
What gravity does, is change what straight

80
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:40,600
means.

81
00:02:41,490 --> 00:02:43,840
Picture a flat sheet, with a grid drawn on

82
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:44,030
it.

83
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:47,040
On this sheet, a straight line is exactly

84
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:48,000
what you expect.

85
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:50,750
Two lines that start side by side, stay

86
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:52,450
side by side forever.

87
00:02:53,420 --> 00:02:55,100
Now put a mass in the middle.

88
00:02:55,260 --> 00:02:57,160
The sheet is no longer flat.

89
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:59,980
Near the mass, distances stretch.

90
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,620
And this funnel is not a cartoon.

91
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:03,940
Its shape is computed.

92
00:03:04,170 --> 00:03:06,580
It is the real geometry of a slice of

93
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:07,810
space around a star.

94
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:11,110
Send two rays across this surface.

95
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,480
Each one goes as straight as it possibly

96
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:14,240
can.

97
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:16,360
It never turns left, and it never turns

98
00:03:16,593 --> 00:03:16,940
right.

99
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:19,790
And yet, the paths bend toward the mass.

100
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:22,260
Nothing pulled on them.

101
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:24,160
The space they moved through was curved,

102
00:03:24,450 --> 00:03:26,580
so the straightest possible path was a

103
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:26,940
curve.

104
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:29,970
Now look from directly above.

105
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:31,760
This is the picture we will use from here

106
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:32,533
on.

107
00:03:32,587 --> 00:03:34,907
A mass at the centre, and a ray of light,

108
00:03:34,933 --> 00:03:36,030
bending as it passes.

109
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:39,060
So the question becomes precise.

110
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:41,780
Exactly what curve does the light follow?

111
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:45,725
Here is a deeper look, for those who want

112
00:03:45,725 --> 00:03:45,840
it.

113
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:47,990
If you would rather keep things simple,

114
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:49,360
hold on for one minute.

115
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:51,750
Everything after this still makes sense.

116
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:54,930
That funnel showed curved space.

117
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:58,010
But Einstein's theory curves time as well.

118
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:00,870
Space and time, woven together.

119
00:04:01,810 --> 00:04:04,298
So let us draw a different map.

120
00:04:04,432 --> 00:04:06,160
Distance from the mass runs across.

121
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:07,810
Time runs upward.

122
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,030
Every point on it is an event.

123
00:04:10,330 --> 00:04:11,760
A place, and a moment.

124
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:14,985
On this map, a flash of light traces a

125
00:04:15,065 --> 00:04:15,430
cone.

126
00:04:15,910 --> 00:04:18,025
Far from the mass, every cone stands

127
00:04:18,105 --> 00:04:18,420
upright.

128
00:04:18,750 --> 00:04:21,145
Light moves outward and inward equally

129
00:04:21,185 --> 00:04:21,570
easily.

130
00:04:22,510 --> 00:04:24,620
The colour shows how fast time runs.

131
00:04:24,870 --> 00:04:27,250
Far away, clocks run at full speed.

132
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:29,590
Closer in, they tick more slowly.

133
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:32,445
And the cones begin to tip, leaning toward

134
00:04:32,525 --> 00:04:33,565
the mass.

135
00:04:33,565 --> 00:04:35,565
At the Schwarzschild radius, the cone has

136
00:04:35,605 --> 00:04:38,045
tipped so far that its outer edge points

137
00:04:38,125 --> 00:04:38,650
straight up.

138
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:41,130
Light aimed outward just hovers there.

139
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:44,380
Inside, every possible path leads inward.

140
00:04:45,290 --> 00:04:47,625
All of this is packed into one formula,

141
00:04:47,665 --> 00:04:48,945
called the metric.

142
00:04:48,945 --> 00:04:50,865
It measures the separation between two

143
00:04:50,945 --> 00:04:51,800
nearby events.

144
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:54,545
And for light, that separation is always

145
00:04:54,625 --> 00:04:54,970
zero.

146
00:04:55,780 --> 00:04:57,925
Set it to zero, follow the light around

147
00:04:58,005 --> 00:05:00,445
the mass, and after a page of calculus,

148
00:05:00,485 --> 00:05:02,510
the path obeys one short equation.

149
00:05:02,950 --> 00:05:04,530
That is where we are going next.

150
00:05:06,750 --> 00:05:08,860
To describe the path, we need two numbers

151
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:10,460
for each point on it.

152
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:12,480
The angle around the mass, called phi.

153
00:05:12,830 --> 00:05:14,920
And the distance from the mass, called r.

154
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:17,760
There is one trick that makes everything

155
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:18,160
simpler.

156
00:05:18,410 --> 00:05:20,720
Instead of the distance, we track one

157
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:22,010
divided by the distance.

158
00:05:22,270 --> 00:05:22,930
Call it u.

159
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:25,920
When the light is far away, u is almost

160
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:26,550
zero.

161
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:28,660
When it comes close, u grows.

162
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:32,233
First, take the mass away completely.

163
00:05:32,287 --> 00:05:33,740
The light goes perfectly straight.

164
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:36,520
And if you plot u against the angle, that

165
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:38,980
straight line becomes a pure sine wave.

166
00:05:39,210 --> 00:05:41,780
It rises as the light approaches, peaks at

167
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:43,670
the closest point, and falls again.

168
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:46,600
A sine wave obeys a famous little

169
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:47,600
equation.

170
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:50,200
Its second derivative, plus itself, equals

171
00:05:50,820 --> 00:05:51,320
zero.

172
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:53,160
So this is the equation of a straight

173
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:54,040
line.

174
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:56,620
U double prime, plus u, equals zero.

175
00:05:57,660 --> 00:05:58,940
Now bring the mass back.

176
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:01,580
Einstein's theory changes this equation in

177
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:03,460
exactly one place.

178
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:06,260
The zero on the right becomes a new term.

179
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:08,620
Three times G times the mass, over the

180
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,820
speed of light squared, times u squared.

181
00:06:11,530 --> 00:06:13,000
That is it.

182
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,120
That is the equation behind every black

183
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:15,720
hole image.

184
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:18,160
Look at what the new term does.

185
00:06:18,490 --> 00:06:19,940
It contains u squared.

186
00:06:20,190 --> 00:06:22,900
Far from the mass, u is tiny, and u

187
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:25,540
squared is far tinier, so the term does

188
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:27,660
nothing, and the light goes straight.

189
00:06:27,870 --> 00:06:29,780
Only close to the mass does it wake up,

190
00:06:29,980 --> 00:06:31,280
and bend the path inward.

191
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:33,747
Let us test it.

192
00:06:33,747 --> 00:06:35,800
A ray of starlight, just grazing the edge

193
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:36,360
of the Sun.

194
00:06:36,590 --> 00:06:39,080
The equation says it bends by one point

195
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:40,520
seven five arc seconds.

196
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:43,080
That is the width of a small coin, seen

197
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:44,340
from two kilometres away.

198
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:47,340
And it is exactly twice what the thrown

199
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:48,600
ball argument predicted.

200
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:51,420
In nineteen nineteen, astronomers measured

201
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:52,900
it during a solar eclipse.

202
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:55,580
The stars near the Sun had shifted, by

203
00:06:55,660 --> 00:06:56,540
just this amount.

204
00:06:57,350 --> 00:06:59,770
So the equation works for a gentle bend.

205
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:02,320
But the Sun is far larger than its

206
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:03,430
Schwarzschild radius.

207
00:07:03,670 --> 00:07:05,520
What happens when we aim the light at

208
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:07,680
something much smaller, and pass much

209
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:08,190
closer?

210
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:12,700
Here is a black hole, and a ray of light

211
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:14,407
passing far from it.

212
00:07:14,407 --> 00:07:15,910
It bends a little, and moves on.

213
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:17,730
Aim closer.

214
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:19,190
The bend grows.

215
00:07:19,590 --> 00:07:20,520
Ninety degrees.

216
00:07:20,990 --> 00:07:21,720
Then more than that.

217
00:07:21,990 --> 00:07:23,840
The light swings around the back of the

218
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:25,800
hole, and heads home the way it came.

219
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:29,220
Closer still, and the ray makes a full

220
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:30,590
loop before it escapes.

221
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:33,880
Before we go on, make a guess.

222
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:36,360
If we keep aiming closer, what is the last

223
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:38,190
thing the light does before it falls in?

224
00:07:39,470 --> 00:07:40,260
It does this.

225
00:07:40,670 --> 00:07:41,520
It circles.

226
00:07:41,770 --> 00:07:44,300
There is one exact distance at which light

227
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:47,030
can orbit a black hole, around and around.

228
00:07:47,810 --> 00:07:49,560
And our equation tells us where.

229
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:52,320
On a perfect circle, the distance never

230
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:52,870
changes.

231
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:55,200
So u never changes, and its second

232
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:56,360
derivative is zero.

233
00:07:56,770 --> 00:07:57,630
Cross that term out.

234
00:07:57,940 --> 00:08:00,480
What remains is simple algebra, and it

235
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:02,640
gives a radius of one and a half times the

236
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:03,720
Schwarzschild radius.

237
00:08:04,610 --> 00:08:06,220
This is the photon sphere.

238
00:08:06,490 --> 00:08:08,190
A shell of orbiting light.

239
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:11,400
The orbit is unstable, like a pencil

240
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:13,000
balanced on its tip.

241
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:14,680
A ray that arrives the slightest bit

242
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:16,170
outside, spirals away.

243
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:18,920
The slightest bit inside, and it spirals

244
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:20,030
in, and is gone.

245
00:08:21,070 --> 00:08:23,470
So every ray has one of three fates.

246
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:26,820
It escapes, it orbits, or it is captured.

247
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:29,660
And what decides its fate, is how far off

248
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:30,650
centre it was aimed.

249
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:33,260
The dividing line sits at about two point

250
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:35,687
six times the Schwarzschild radius.

251
00:08:36,187 --> 00:08:37,640
That number matters.

252
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:39,600
It means the dark region is bigger than

253
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:40,460
the hole itself.

254
00:08:41,030 --> 00:08:43,440
Any light aimed inside that circle never

255
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:44,120
comes back out.

256
00:08:46,190 --> 00:08:47,710
One more deeper look.

257
00:08:48,170 --> 00:08:49,920
Why exactly three fates?

258
00:08:51,010 --> 00:08:53,160
Rewrite the equation as a ball rolling

259
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:54,110
over a landscape.

260
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:56,440
The height of the land depends on the

261
00:08:56,640 --> 00:08:58,173
distance from the hole.

262
00:08:58,227 --> 00:09:00,760
It rises to a single peak, and that peak

263
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:03,440
sits exactly at the photon sphere.

264
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:05,920
A ray of light comes in from far away,

265
00:09:06,220 --> 00:09:07,820
like a ball rolling toward the hill.

266
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:10,680
How high it rides depends on its aim.

267
00:09:11,240 --> 00:09:13,530
A wide aim means a low ball.

268
00:09:13,770 --> 00:09:16,250
It climbs, stops, and rolls back out.

269
00:09:16,570 --> 00:09:18,060
That ray escapes.

270
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:22,080
Aim closer, and the ball rides higher.

271
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:24,640
At one precise height, it reaches the very

272
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:26,120
top, and balances there.

273
00:09:26,570 --> 00:09:28,560
That is the orbit.

274
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:31,060
Any higher, and it clears the peak, and

275
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:33,290
falls down the far side, into the hole.

276
00:09:33,810 --> 00:09:34,320
Captured.

277
00:09:35,340 --> 00:09:35,960
One hill.

278
00:09:36,290 --> 00:09:37,240
Three outcomes.

279
00:09:37,450 --> 00:09:39,400
And the height of that peak is what sets

280
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:41,260
the number two point six.

281
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:43,980
We now have everything we need.

282
00:09:44,310 --> 00:09:46,200
One equation, and three fates.

283
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:47,630
Let us build the picture.

284
00:09:49,690 --> 00:09:51,550
Put a camera near the black hole.

285
00:09:51,770 --> 00:09:53,880
Each pixel of its image looks out in one

286
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:54,867
direction.

287
00:09:54,893 --> 00:09:57,080
And for each pixel, we ask one question.

288
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,040
What light arrives from there?

289
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:02,080
To answer, we run the light backwards.

290
00:10:02,310 --> 00:10:04,780
Start a ray at the pixel, send it out from

291
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:06,810
the camera, and let the equation bend it.

292
00:10:07,330 --> 00:10:08,730
Then we see where it ends up.

293
00:10:09,710 --> 00:10:11,390
Some rays fall into the hole.

294
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:13,600
No light can come from there, so those

295
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:15,520
pixels stay black.

296
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:17,280
Together they form the shadow, and its

297
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:19,230
edge is that dividing line from before.

298
00:10:20,020 --> 00:10:22,410
Some rays escape to the distant stars.

299
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:25,140
But they arrive bent, so the pixel shows a

300
00:10:25,220 --> 00:10:27,000
star from a different part of the sky.

301
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:29,540
Around the hole, the whole sky is

302
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:31,660
stretched, and wrapped into rings.

303
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:33,910
Now add the disk.

304
00:10:34,210 --> 00:10:37,160
A flat, thin ring of glowing gas, circling

305
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:37,580
the hole.

306
00:10:38,550 --> 00:10:40,300
Rays that hit the front of the disk give

307
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:41,950
this bright band across the middle.

308
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:43,300
Nothing surprising yet.

309
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:46,240
But follow a ray that leaves the camera

310
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:47,780
aimed just above the hole.

311
00:10:48,120 --> 00:10:51,080
It bends over the top, comes down behind,

312
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:53,000
and lands on the far side of the disk.

313
00:10:53,510 --> 00:10:55,680
So above the hole, you see the back of the

314
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:57,160
disk, lifted into view.

315
00:10:58,070 --> 00:10:59,750
That is the arc from the beginning.

316
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:00,890
Not a second ring.

317
00:11:01,350 --> 00:11:03,580
The same disk, seen over the top of the

318
00:11:03,620 --> 00:11:03,870
hole.

319
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:06,420
And beneath it, the underside, seen from

320
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:06,770
below.

321
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:10,360
Hugging the shadow is one more line, thin

322
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:10,970
and sharp.

323
00:11:11,370 --> 00:11:13,240
That is light that circled the photon

324
00:11:13,320 --> 00:11:15,140
sphere, before escaping toward us.

325
00:11:16,030 --> 00:11:17,870
And one side is brighter than the other.

326
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:20,540
The gas on that side is rushing toward the

327
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:22,620
camera, at a large fraction of the speed

328
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:22,990
of light.

329
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:25,100
And motion toward you makes light

330
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:25,460
brighter.

331
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:28,140
Every feature has a reason now.

332
00:11:30,150 --> 00:11:32,300
In twenty nineteen, a telescope the size

333
00:11:32,380 --> 00:11:34,260
of the Earth took a real picture of a

334
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:36,780
black hole, fifty five million light years

335
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:37,140
away.

336
00:11:37,620 --> 00:11:40,060
It showed a dark shadow, wrapped in a ring

337
00:11:40,180 --> 00:11:40,760
of bent light.

338
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:42,400
Just as the equation said.

339
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:45,600
So here is what a picture of a black hole

340
00:11:45,680 --> 00:11:46,360
really is.

341
00:11:46,740 --> 00:11:49,040
It is a map of where straight lines go, in

342
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:50,480
a space that is curved.

343
00:11:51,390 --> 00:11:53,170
A speed limit gave us the radius.

344
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:55,240
Curved space gave us the idea.

345
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:57,520
One extra term gave us the path.

346
00:11:57,800 --> 00:11:59,390
And the path gave us the picture.

347
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:01,280
One thing we left out.

348
00:12:01,710 --> 00:12:04,200
Real black holes spin, and a spinning hole

349
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:05,530
drags space around with it.

350
00:12:05,890 --> 00:12:07,360
The shadow becomes lopsided.

351
00:12:07,750 --> 00:12:10,390
That is a story for the next video.
